finish('public-key-encryption-from-arbitrary-one-way-functions',
 id='TCS-6545', title='Do one-way functions imply public-key encryption in the standard model?',
 area='Cryptography', criterion='assumptions', question_type='yes_no',
 formal=r'''Does the existence of a classical cryptographic one-way function imply the existence of a classical public-key encryption scheme with negligible correctness error and security against uniform probabilistic polynomial-time chosen-plaintext adversaries? General constructions and security proofs may use the code of the one-way function; they need not be black-box.''',
 definitions=r'''A one-way function is a deterministic polynomial-time map f:{0,1}*→{0,1}* such that every uniform classical PPT inverter I has negligible probability, for x uniform in {0,1}ⁿ, of outputting z∈{0,1}ⁿ with f(z)=f(x) on input (1ⁿ,f(x)). A function ν(n) is negligible if for every d>0 it is eventually at most n^{−d}. The scheme comprises uniform classical polynomial-time algorithms Gen, Enc, Dec. Gen(1ⁿ) randomly outputs public and secret bit strings (pk,sk); Enc(1ⁿ,pk,b) randomly encrypts a bit b; Dec(1ⁿ,sk,c) deterministically outputs a bit or failure. For each b, the probability over honest generation and encryption that decryption is wrong is negligible. Security means that for every uniform classical PPT adversary A, in the experiment (pk,sk)←Gen(1ⁿ), b←{0,1}, c←Enc(1ⁿ,pk,b), the advantage |Pr[A(1ⁿ,pk,c)=b]−1/2| is negligible. The adversary knows all algorithms and can encrypt arbitrary chosen bits itself with fresh randomness. This bit-message formulation extends to polynomially long messages by independent bit encryption and a hybrid argument. All runtime bounds are worst-case polynomial in the security parameter and appropriate input lengths. There is no shared secret before key generation, trusted setup, random oracle, quantum state, or auxiliary hardness assumption. Security is eventually negligible at all sufficiently large parameters, not merely infinitely often.''',
 answer_criterion='Answer yes by proving the standard-model implication from existence of an OWF alone, allowing unrestricted non-black-box techniques. A negative answer must refute that implication in the stated model. An oracle separation or impossibility theorem for black-box reductions alone is insufficient, as is showing that one particular candidate construction is insecure.',
 context=[
  paragraph('A one-way function creates an asymmetry between evaluating a map and finding a preimage of a random image. Public-key encryption needs a different asymmetry: anyone holding the public information can produce ciphertexts, yet a receiver possessing related secret information can recover their messages. The question is whether this additional structure is implicit in every possible source of one-wayness.','survey'),
  paragraph('One-way functions already suffice for pseudorandom generators and, through the standard chain of constructions, private-key encryption. There the honest parties initially share a secret. Public-key encryption removes that initial shared secret, so the existence of symmetric cryptography is not already a construction of the desired public-key primitive.','hill'),
  paragraph('The classical obstacle is the Impagliazzo–Rudich black-box separation. Roughly, an idealized one-way-function oracle can supply private-key cryptographic hardness without enabling the necessary key agreement. A construction allowed to inspect and reason about a concrete function’s program lies outside the oracle interface, which is why the general implication remains open despite this barrier.','barrier'),
  paragraph('Public-key encryption would give a two-message key-agreement protocol: send a public key, then send an encryption of a freshly chosen shared secret. Barak and Mahmoody show a quadratic-query attack on key agreement built from a random oracle, matching the limited query advantage of Merkle puzzles. The theorem counts oracle queries and allows extensive other computation; it is not an unconditional attack on every standard-model encryption scheme.','quadratic'),
  paragraph('New constructions continue to broaden the available assumptions. Chatterjee, Mu, and Vasudevan base public-key encryption on a planted MinRank problem over random instances, equivalently a rank-metric decoding assumption, rather than hiding a specially structured efficiently decodable code. This is progress in the choice of concrete hardness assumptions. It still assumes that specific problem is hard, a property not supplied merely by the existence of some arbitrary OWF.','minrank')],
 why='This is the main unresolved boundary between the assumptions needed for private-key and public-key cryptography. It asks whether generic computational one-wayness already contains the structure needed to establish encrypted communication without a pre-shared secret.',
 importance=dict(score=98, method='editorial', assessed_on='2026-09-10', reason='A central minimal-assumption question in cryptographic foundations, separating the known reach of generic one-wayness from public-key communication.'),
 progress=[
  step('1989','Impagliazzo and Rudich establish the fundamental oracle and black-box barriers to obtaining key agreement from one-way permutations; this also constrains public-key encryption constructions.','barrier'),
  step('1999','Håstad, Impagliazzo, Levin, and Luby prove pseudorandom generators can be constructed from any OWF, establishing the generic route underlying private-key cryptography.','hill'),
  step('2009','Barak and Mahmoody prove the quadratic-query attack on random-oracle key agreement. The result sharpens the oracle-model limitation without excluding arbitrary non-black-box constructions.','quadratic'),
  step('2023','MIT’s foundations lecture explicitly presents PKE from OWFs as a major open question and distinguishes it from the established black-box separations.','lecture'),
  step('2025–2026','Chatterjee, Mu, and Vasudevan construct PKE from planted MinRank hardness on random instances. The 2025 preprint appears at EUROCRYPT 2026; it adds a concrete foundation, not a reduction from an arbitrary OWF.','minrank')],
 references=[
  ref('lecture','Foundations of Cryptography, Lecture 10','MIT 6.875',2023,'https://mit6875.github.io/FA23SLIDES/lec10.pdf','Fall 2023 lecture; epilogue slide “A Big Open Question,” PKE from OWFs and black-box separations'),
  ref('survey','The Complexity of Public-Key Cryptography','Boaz Barak',2017,'https://eprint.iacr.org/2017/365','Tutorials on the Foundations of Cryptography, 45–77; survey of assumptions and evidence; DOI 10.1007/978-3-319-57048-8_2'),
  ref('barrier','Limits on the provable consequences of one-way permutations','Russell Impagliazzo; Steven Rudich',1989,'https://doi.org/10.1145/73007.73012','STOC 1989, 44–61; oracle and black-box barriers for key agreement'),
  ref('hill','A Pseudorandom Generator from any One-way Function','Johan Håstad; Russell Impagliazzo; Leonid A. Levin; Michael Luby',1999,'https://johanhastad.se/prgfromowf.pdf','SIAM Journal on Computing 28(4), 1364–1396; DOI 10.1137/S0097539793244708'),
  ref('quadratic','Merkle Puzzles are Optimal — an O(n²)-query attack on any key exchange from a random oracle','Boaz Barak; Mohammad Mahmoody-Ghidary',2009,'https://www.boazbarak.org/Papers/merkle.pdf','Author version 18 April 2009; CRYPTO 2009, 374–390; oracle-query complexity, not arbitrary standard-model attack time'),
  ref('minrank','Public-Key Encryption from the MinRank Problem','Rohit Chatterjee; Changrui Mu; Prashant Nalini Vasudevan',2026,'https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03752','Preprint 4 October 2025; EUROCRYPT 2026 Part IV, 449–480; hardness of planted MinRank on uniformly random instances')],
 status='source_open', status_note='Explicitly open in the supplied MIT lecture. Research through 10 September 2026 found no standard-model implication from arbitrary OWFs alone or refutation of that general implication. The formulation fixes uniform classical adversaries and allows non-black-box constructions; oracle barriers and stronger concrete assumptions are kept separate.', formulation_reviewed_on='2026-09-10')
